{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788131518,"nonce":"J+XiE58Rm8TefWm8xPsOa8Y8Mi8=","message":"CLOSED — orchestrator verification, session 4, dorian's Linux box. Merged to\n`main` fast-forward `09d1a1b..7e83f9b` (8 source commits + 1 standalone\n`Build:`). The merge was a true fast-forward and the merged tree's\n`Assemblies/AutoRimmer.dll` is blob-identical to the branch tip's\n(`7be504a`), so the branch build IS the shipped artifact and no rebuild is\nowed. Committed DLL: pdb path names this worktree's `obj/Release/`,\n`AssemblyConfiguration` blob Release with no Debug blob,\n`InformationalVersion 1.0.0+10ff739…` = the commit below the `Build:` commit.\n\nEverything below is MY run against the committed artifact on a fresh\n`--quicktest` bench, not the worker's. Every headline number re-derived from\nraw output rather than read off a summary.\n\n\n**B1 find-rect selection.** Verified by re-derivation, not by eyeball. For 9\n(near, w×h) combinations I recomputed `center` from `at + (w/2,h/2)` and `dist`\nfrom `hypot(near, center)` independently: zero mismatches, ordering monotonic\nascending in all 9. Where the ideal centre is clear the walk now returns\n`dist 0.0` exactly:\n\n    find-rect {w:5,h:7,near:[120,130],max:5}\n      at=[118,127] center=[120,130] dist=0\n      at=[118,126] center=[120,129] dist=1     … searched_radius=1 capped=False\n\nThe differential test that 2.3 would have failed — top-1 must not depend on how\nmany you ask for:\n\n    max= 1 → at=[119,122] dist=4   searched_radius=4\n    max= 3 → at=[119,122] dist=4   searched_radius=4\n    max= 5 → at=[119,122] dist=4   searched_radius=5\n    max=20 → at=[119,122] dist=4   searched_radius=6\n\nCross-checked that the nearer rect it did NOT return is genuinely blocked: a\nsecond query centred on that rect (`near=[127,128]`) also refuses it and tallies\n`edifice-in-way: 46`. Two independent queries agree.\n\n**B2 alert severity before truncation.** 15 fixture alerts injected with the\nCritical LAST (readout index 19 — precisely the one `live[0..11]` dropped), on\ntop of 5 real ones:\n\n    total=20 shown=12 more=8 more_by_priority={'Medium': 8}\n      0 Critical Alert_AutoRimmerFixtureCritical  [fixture] critical #15\n      1 High     Alert_NeedWarmClothes\n      2 High     Alert_NeedColonistBeds\n      3 Medium   Alert_NeedResearchBench …\n\nEvery one of the 8 truncations is Medium; discovery order is preserved within a\npriority (real alerts ahead of fixtures, fixtures #1..#6 in order), so the\ntie-break is doing its job. Later, on state the storyteller generated by itself\nrather than my fixture, `Alert_ColonistNeedsRescuing / Critical` came back as\n`active[0]` — the exact alert the issue named as the one that could be hidden.\n\n**B3 colonist cap.** 20 colonists spawned:\n\n    capped   digest 2020 B   colonists 966 B  shown 10 of 20  more 10  order attention-desc\n    uncapped digest 2945 B   colonists 1891 B shown 20\n\n**This acceptance bullet is met in a weaker form than its text asks.** The\nbullet says \"keeps the digest inside budget\". What is actually true is that the\ndigest is now BOUNDED where it was unbounded — the real defect is fixed — but\nit is not inside DESIGN's \"~1–2KB\". With both caps saturated I measured:\n\n    WORST CASE (20 colonists, 20 alerts): 2625 B\n      time 134 · alerts 1053 · colonists 966 · resources 132 · power 114 ·\n      threats 41 · changed 109\n\nAt full caps the dominant term is now `alerts` (1053 B), not colonists — and the\nalert cap of 12 is 2.1's, untouched by this spec. So 2.6 capped the right thing\nand the residual budget breach moved rather than closed. Filed as a follow-up\nrather than held against this issue.\n\n**B4 map-view radius 30.**\n\n    radius 12 → ok 25x25 | radius 29 → ok 59x59 | radius 30 → ok 61x61 (61 rows)\n    radius 31 → bad-args \"radius must be 1..30\"\n\n\n**Fog.** `map-view` at the unexplored corner renders 242 `?` cells with\n`legend {\"?\": \"unexplored (fogged)\"}`; `find-rect` there tallies `fogged: 98`\nand reports it as a blocker with `removal none / reason 'unexplored'`;\n`room-at {at:[240,240]}` returns `{\"fogged\": true, \"room\": null}` and leaks no\nroom detail.\n\n**Removal taxonomy — all four values observed live, each with the game's own\nstring**, across three map locations:\n\n    fogged                  removal none         reason 'unexplored'\n    Marble                  removal mine                                  (natural rock)\n    Wall / Door / Battery   removal deconstruct                           (player faction)\n    AncientWarwalkerTorso   removal attack       reason 'Remove this by attacking it.'\n    AncientRustedCarFrame   removal attack       reason 'Remove this by attacking it.'\n\nI checked that string against the game's own keyed XML, not just the code:\n`Core/Languages/English/Keyed/Misc_Gameplay.xml:284` defines\n`RemoveByAttackingTooltip` as exactly \"Remove this by attacking it.\" Marble\nlanding on `mine` rather than `none` is the subtle one and it is right —\n`BuildingProperties.IsDeconstructible` (decompiled `:436`) returns false for\n`isNaturalRock`, so a DeconstructibleBy-first ordering would have mislabelled\nevery mountain on the map as permanent.\n\nThe worker also handled a trap the issue did not mention: `DeconstructibleBy`\nreturns a bare `false` for `!IsDeconstructible`, and `AcceptanceReport(false)\n.Reason` is `\"\"`. `Blockers.Classify` emits `reason: null` there rather than an\nempty string dressed up as the game's words. It also documents that\n`DebugSettings.godMode` makes `DeconstructibleBy` accept everything (godMode is\noff on the bench and is not `Prefs.DevMode`).\n\n\n**SF5 power.** Gen and draw are separable, and the three figures are mutually\nconsistent — which is the whole point, since `gain_w` alone cannot distinguish\n0−60 from 1000−1060:\n\n    unfuelled+settling: gen_w 1000  draw_w 0   (lamp not yet drawing)\n    after +600 ticks  : gen_w 1000  draw_w 30  gain_w 970   ( = 1000-30 )\n    after fuel ran out: gen_w 0     draw_w 60  gain_w -60   stored_wd 1157\n                        battery_days 19.28\n\nRe-derived: 1157 / (60−0) = 19.283 → published 19.28. The `draw_w 0` at\nsettling time is not a defect — a just-spawned lamp has not been picked up by\nthe power net yet, and it reaches the fixture's own `expect_draw_w 30` after\n600 ticks. I also confirmed the rider's premise directly in the decompiled\nsource: `PowerNet.IsPowerSource` returns true for a bare `CompPowerBattery`\n(`:96-107`), so 2.1's `nets` really does count a battery-only grid as powered,\nand `nets_with_generator` is the strict test.\n\n**SF6 glyph band.** Pawns and fog now own `? @ $ ! \u0026 ^ ;`. A live crop shows `;`\n(wild animal) coexisting with `s`/`t`/`m` items and no merged legend entry;\nunder 2.3 the same crop would have collided `g`/`d`/`w` with gold/duster/wood.\n\n**Nits verified:** `nearest {def:\"MinifiedThing\"}` returns ok with 0 hits and\nemits NO red error; a fully off-map rect is now `bad-args` (\"lies entirely\noutside the 250x250 map\") while a partially overlapping one still clips honestly\n(`clipped:true origin=[0,0] 7x7`); `since` reads as `long`.\n\n\nOne 240,000-tick advance (4 in-game days) on a 20-colonist colony, `halt_on_error`\nat its default of true. It ran to completion without halting:\n\n    reason ticks · ticks_elapsed 240000 · wall 343.9s · avg_tps 697.8\n    11 TimeSlower spans reported-not-honored · thermal_scale 0.5\n\nSession journal over the whole run: 19 deaths, 21 downed, 16 mental breaks, 45\nletters, **0 `red_error` events**. LogRelay: **0 errors / 9 warnings**, the exact\nFINDINGS §5 benign set. `Mono path[0]` profile-rooted; 0 exceptions in Player.log.\n\nIncidental first: the thermal governor ENGAGED, which BORGES can never test (no\nsensor). It held `scale 0.5` from ~93.5 °C for most of the run and tps settled at\n~700 against the 1000 cap. Short bursts still overshoot briefly (a 3000-tick\nadvance reported 1042 tps), consistent with session 3's finding.\n\n\n- **SF7, designations rendering over things — I did not demonstrate this.** No\n  shipped verb creates a designation; 3.2 owns `designate`. Diff-review only,\n  same as the worker reported.\n- **`threats.kinds` count-descending ordering — I did not demonstrate this.**\n  There were zero hostiles alive at every point I sampled, so `kinds` was `[]`.\n- **`nets_with_generator: 0` on a truly isolated battery-only grid — I did not\n  demonstrate this.** My second fixture grid merged into the first via touching\n  conduits (`nets: 1`), so I never had an isolated one. The premise is verified\n  from decompiled source; the observation is not.\n- **The in-game power tab — I did not demonstrate this.** The acceptance line\n  names it and reading it needs a human at the screen.\n- Save/load of anything in this spec (nothing here adds persisted state).\n\n\nFour fixture steps on `journal-selftest` (`alerts`, `alerts-clear`, `colonists`,\n`power`), declared by the worker in comment #4 with the same disclosure duty as\n2.1's `stockpile` step: `Prefs.DevMode`-gated, one journalled `dev` event each,\nsuperseded by 3.1. I verified `alerts-clear` reverses `alerts` exactly (20 → 5\nactive). The injection is safe by construction rather than by luck: I confirmed\nin the decompiled source that `AlertsReadout` excludes `Alert_Custom` /\n`Alert_CustomCritical` subclasses from `allAlertTypesCached` (`:64`), so the\nround-robin never instantiates or removes an injected instance, and the only\npath that removes from `activeAlerts` during update is an exception from\n`AlertActiveUpdate` (`:226-238`).","files":null}]}